//! The reference client's icon set, compiled into egui shapes.
//!
//! **Generated by `tools/gen_icons_egui.py`. Do not edit.**
//!
//! This is the egui twin of `crates/sift-desktop/src/icons_gen.rs`: the
//! same 441 SVGs out of `vendor/modrinth-app/assets/icons`, read by
//! the same parser, listed in the same order under the same names, so the two
//! files can be diffed a glyph at a time. The parser is not copied --
//! `tools/gen_icons_egui.py` imports `tools/gen_icons.py` and calls its
//! `read_icon` -- because two SVG readers in one tree is two answers to what
//! an icon looks like, and only one of them gets fixed.
//!
//! | table | where | variants | glyphs |
//! | --- | --- | --- | --- |
//! | `ALL` | `icons/` | `none` | 313 |
//! | `TAG_CATEGORIES` | `icons/tags/categories` | `TagCategory` | 102 |
//! | `TAG_LOADERS` | `icons/tags/loaders` | `TagLoader` | 26 |
//!
//! The prefixes are the reference's own, and the four tag icons the parser
//! refuses are absent here for the same reason they are absent there:
//!
//! * `tags/loaders/geyser.svg` -- non-uniform transform scale (1.0404 x 1.1631)
//! * `tags/loaders/legacy-fabric.svg` -- unknown attribute `clip-path` on <g>
//! * `tags/loaders/purpur.svg` -- non-uniform transform scale (1.1250 x 1.1372)
//! * `tags/loaders/quilt.svg` -- non-uniform transform scale (0.0305 x 0.0305)
//!
//! ## What is different from the iced file
//!
//! **Shapes, not strings.** iced's path builder holds subpaths and cubic
//! Beziers; every epaint primitive holds finished points. So the generator
//! flattens every cubic at generation time -- recursive halving until both
//! control points are within 0.002 of the view box's long side
//! of the chord, which is 0.05 px at 24 px and 0.1 px at 48 px, well
//! under epaint's half-pixel feathering -- and every coordinate in this file
//! is a plain number: 58,805 of them, none computed twice.
//!
//! **The fills are triangulated, holes included.** `PathShape` has a fill
//! colour and no fill rule, and epaint tessellates it with a triangle fan from
//! its first point: exact only for a contour that is star-shaped from that
//! point, and with no way to leave a hole empty -- the fan paints a nested
//! contour solid. So every filled element is triangulated here, once, by ear
//! clipping with hole bridging, the construction SVG's fill rules are defined
//! against (the one mapbox's `earcut` implements). Each contour is wound the
//! same way and its nesting measured; the element's own `fill-rule` then
//! decides what is filled -- `evenodd` by nesting parity, `nonzero` by winding
//! number -- and each hole is cut into its outer contour by a bridge to the
//! nearest visible vertex. The corridor is walked twice, so it has no width
//! and adds no area: the triangles sum to the outer contour minus its holes.
//! The result is a `Shape::Mesh`, and a stroke stays a `PathShape`.
//! 1,537 of the 3,146 shapes are meshes: 58 filled,
//! 57 dots and 1,422 round-join meshes holding
//! 4,628 discs and half-discs, with 27,868 triangles in total.
//!
//! **Dots are discs.** 57 stroked subpaths in the set are shorter than a
//! thousandth of their view box -- the reference's dot idiom, a zero-length
//! line whose round cap is the whole of the picture (`M12 12h.01`) -- and
//! epaint has no round caps, so a stroke of one draws nothing at all. Each is
//! emitted as the disc that cap would have painted: a regular polygon of the
//! stroke's width, filled in the stroke's ink, marked `dot` in the tables.
//! 34 glyphs have at least one; a dot that vanished is the kind of
//! difference nobody finds by reading coordinates.
//!
//! 17 of the 33 filled elements, in 16 glyphs,
//! could not have been drawn by that fan -- a concave outline, a hole, or
//! both. They are exact now, and this is what each one costs:
//!
//! * `cloud.svg` -- 63 triangles
//! * `cog.svg` -- 110 triangles (1 hole)
//! * `online-indicator.svg` -- 128 triangles (1 hole)
//! * `radio-button.svg` -- 128 triangles (1 hole)
//! * `radio-button-checked.svg` -- 128 triangles (1 hole)
//! * `spinner.svg` -- 44 triangles
//! * `tags/categories/entities.svg` -- 14 triangles
//! * `tags/categories/mobs.svg` -- 14 triangles
//! * `tags/categories/potato.svg` -- 116 triangles (1 hole)
//! * `tags/loaders/babric.svg` -- 51 triangles (2 holes)
//! * `tags/loaders/bta-babric.svg` -- 51 triangles (2 holes)
//! * `tags/loaders/datapack.svg` -- 222 triangles
//! * `tags/loaders/minecraft.svg` -- 222 triangles
//! * `tags/loaders/vanilla.svg` -- 222 triangles
//! * `tags/loaders/velocity.svg` -- 101 triangles (1 hole)
//! * `x.svg` -- 42 triangles
//!
//! The iced side carries the reference's `fill-rule` and honours it, and so
//! does this file: the rule is read off the element and applied by the
//! triangulator rather than approximated away.
//!
//! **10 elements are not here at all.** They are the shapes the
//! reference declares with neither a fill nor a stroke -- the clamp path every
//! exported icon carries, `M0 0h24v24H0Z` -- which SVG paints nothing for. The
//! iced file emits them as `Cmd::NoPaint` so a reader can see it read them; a
//! shape that paints nothing has no `PathShape` to be, so they are dropped and
//! named here instead:
//!
//! * `key.svg` element 1
//! * `loader.svg` element 1
//! * `palette.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/categories/entities.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/categories/mobs.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/loaders/bungeecord.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/loaders/fabric.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/loaders/forge.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/loaders/liteloader.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/loaders/paper.svg` element 1
//!
//! **Round joins and caps are discs.** `PathStroke` carries a width, a colour
//! and a `StrokeKind`, and no line cap or join; epaint miters every corner and
//! butts every line end. The reference declares
//! `stroke-linecap="round"`/`stroke-linejoin="round"` on 413 of the 441
//! glyphs, so every stroked subpath that turns or ends is followed by one
//! small mesh of round pieces -- the same no-attribute problem as the holes,
//! and the second entry in that parity-log paragraph:
//!
//! * 4,628 discs at 2,226 corners whose turn exceeds ~20 degrees,
//!   and 2,402 half-discs at open ends, over 1,422 stroked subpaths
//!   in 415 glyphs. A closed subpath takes joins but no caps.
//!
//! A disc of the stroke's radius, centred on the vertex, is the arc SVG would
//! have painted there; it is drawn over epaint's miter, not instead of it.
//! What is left is the miter's tip where a corner is sharp enough to push it
//! past the disc (a right angle puts it 41% of the radius beyond): the path
//! has already painted that triangle before the disc goes over it, and a
//! shape cannot erase what it did not paint.
//!
//! ## Performance
//!
//! These shapes are drawn every frame a window draws, so the cost model matters
//! more than the last decimal of a coordinate.
//!
//! * **The geometry is `&'static`.** No SVG is parsed at runtime, no path is
//!   built from a string, and the coordinates and triangle indices live in
//!   the binary. Drawing a glyph allocates one `Vec<Shape>`, one `Vec<Pos2>`
//!   per stroke and one `Mesh` per filled or round piece -- each pre-sized
//!   with `Vec::with_capacity` -- and nothing else.
//! * **One paint-list entry per glyph, not one per path.** `draw` and `cached`
//!   hand egui a single `Shape::Vec`, so a 15-shape glyph costs one entry in
//!   the layer's paint list and one clip decision rather than fifteen, and the
//!   tessellator walks a contiguous batch.
//! * **What is *not* saved: tessellation.** egui caches tessellation for one
//!   frame and then throws it away -- there is no cross-frame mesh cache in the
//!   toolkit -- so a glyph that moves is tessellated again next frame. The cost
//!   this module removes is the allocation, the path building and the
//!   paint-list churn, not the triangles. `cached` goes one step further and
//!   keeps the built shapes across frames for a glyph that has not changed:
//!   a hit is a `Vec` clone, which is a memcpy of the points that were going to
//!   be computed anyway, and a miss is the same build `draw` does.
//!
//! The meshes here are built per draw from view-box coordinates, not baked
//! into absolute positions. Building a mesh skips epaint's tessellation, but
//! egui bakes *absolute* positions into one and has no per-shape transform at
//! paint time, so a cache of baked meshes would have to include the widget's
//! position in its key. A scrolling list moves its icons every frame; every
//! one of them would miss, rebuild its mesh per icon per frame, and the
//! bounded cache would clear itself continuously. Building in view-box
//! coordinates keeps the key the glyph, the fit and the ink -- the same three
//! things a path shape ever needed.

// Nothing draws these yet: `crates/sift-ui` is the first slice of the
// move from iced to egui (see its `Cargo.toml`), and it is a frame-time
// harness rather than the launcher. Delete this line the day the last icon is
// drawn from here.
#![allow(dead_code)]
// Two icon paths round to coordinates of 3.141 and 3.142, which clippy reads as
// approximations of pi. They are geometry inside a view box, not a numeric constant,
// and changing them to satisfy the lint would move the drawing.
#![allow(clippy::approx_constant)]

use std::cell::RefCell;

use egui::epaint::{Mesh, PathShape, PathStroke, Vertex};
use egui::layers::ShapeIdx;
use egui::{Color32, Painter, Pos2, Rect, Shape, Vec2};

/// Where a subpath's colour comes from.
///
/// The same three answers the iced file gives, because they are properties of
/// the reference's SVGs rather than of the toolkit: `currentColor` is what
/// almost every shape in the set asks for, a literal is a shape painted red or
/// white on purpose, and a `var(--token)` is one the shell resolves.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Ink {
    /// `currentColor`: the colour of whatever the icon is drawn in.
    Inherit,
    /// A literal the reference wrote out.
    Fixed(u8, u8, u8, u8),
    /// A `var(--token)` the reference names.
    Token(&'static str),
}

impl Ink {
    /// The colour this shape is, given what the caller is drawing with.
    pub fn resolve(self, inherit: Color32) -> Color32 {
        match self {
            Ink::Inherit => inherit,
            Ink::Fixed(r, g, b, a) => Color32::from_rgba_unmultiplied(r, g, b, a),
            // A token the shell has not been taught yet is drawn in the ink it
            // would have had anyway, which is visible rather than invisible.
            Ink::Token(_) => inherit,
        }
    }
}

/// One drawable piece of an icon: points and how to paint them.
///
/// This is one *shape*, not one SVG element. An element with three stroked
/// subpaths is three of these, because a `PathShape` cannot hold two; a filled
/// element is one per filled region, because its contours are triangulated
/// together so a hole stays empty -- see the module documentation. The iced
/// file's `Element` is the element-level record; this is the shape-level one.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq)]
pub struct Subpath {
    /// The polyline, in the icon's own view box, as x/y pairs.
    ///
    /// For a filled subpath these are the mesh's vertices: the outer contour
    /// followed by each hole, with a bridge repeated rather than duplicated.
    pub points: &'static [(f32, f32)],
    /// For a filled subpath, the triangles of `points`, three indices each.
    ///
    /// Empty for a stroke. The indices are why a fill is a `Shape::Mesh` and
    /// not a `PathShape`: they are the holes and concavities epaint's triangle
    /// fan could not express.
    pub triangles: &'static [u32],
    /// Whether it closes back to its first point. True for every filled one.
    pub closed: bool,
    /// Stroke width in view-box units, before the fit scale is applied.
    pub stroke_width: f32,
    /// Whether this is filled rather than stroked.
    pub filled: bool,
    /// Whether this is one of the reference's dots: a zero-length stroke whose
    /// round cap was the whole picture, emitted as the disc that cap painted.
    ///
    /// Every dot is `filled` and `closed`, and its points are a regular polygon
    /// around the cap's centre. It exists as a field rather than as a quiet
    /// property of the points so a reader can tell a converted dot from a fill.
    pub dot: bool,
    /// How many round joins and caps this mesh holds, or 0.
    ///
    /// A round-join mesh is `filled` and `closed`, always follows the stroked
    /// subpath it belongs to, and exists because neither `PathStroke` nor
    /// epaint's tessellator has a cap or join to set. Like `dot`, it is a field
    /// so a reader and a test can tell it from a filled glyph.
    pub round: u32,
    /// Where its colour comes from.
    pub ink: Ink,
    /// The element's own `opacity`, which multiplies whatever ink it gets.
    pub opacity: f32,
}

